+# Summary: in-repo config parser replaces `inilite`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `confkit/parse.py`: the `inilite` import is gone. `loads()` and `load()` are now a full parser for `docs/FORMAT.md`: lines and comments, dotted names, sections, entries, multi-line values, typed scalars, quoted strings with escapes, lists, `@include` with cycle detection, and `ParseError` with line numbers and source names. Only the first error is reported.
+- `tests/test_format.py` (new): 43 tests for the parts of the spec the existing tests do not cover (typed values, escapes, list errors, section and key conflicts, CRLF/BOM, includes, and CLI exit codes). No existing test was edited or deleted.
+- `SUMMARY.md` (this file).
+
+Nothing else in the repo was changed. `vendor/` is untouched and is not imported. No dependencies were added.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python3 -m unittest` (Python 3.14.3, run from the project root): **56 tests, OK**. That is the 13 existing tests plus 43 new ones.
+- `python3 -m confkit <file>` and `--get` are covered by the CLI tests in `tests/test_format.py`. They check the JSON output, exit 1 with the `file:line: message` text for a parse error, and exit 2 with `no such key: ...` for a missing `--get` path.
+- `git status`: only `confkit/parse.py` (modified) and `tests/test_format.py` (new) are changed.
+
+The expected values in the tests come from reading `docs/FORMAT.md`, not from any reference implementation.
+
+## What I did not verify, and where I am unsure
+
+- **No independent reference.** There is no second implementation to diff against, and I did not fuzz the parser.
+- **Choices where the spec is ambiguous:**
+  - A value whose first character is `#` or `;` right after `=` (e.g. `k =#x`) is treated as a comment, because the value text starts after `=`. If the spec means the whole line, this is wrong.
+  - Inside a list, text directly after a quoted item (`["a"b]`, `["a" "b"]`) is reported as `invalid list`. The spec says "as above" for quoted items, which could also mean `unexpected text after string`.
+  - An unquoted list token is one token up to `,`, `]`, end of line, or comment, so `[1 2]` is the string `"1 2"`. I read the spec's token definition literally.
+  - A trailing `\` at the end of a double-quoted string is reported as `unterminated string`, not `invalid escape`.
+  - Any malformed `@include` argument (bad quote, bad escape, trailing text) is reported as `invalid include`, not as the string error.
+- **Not specified, left as is:** a root file that fails UTF-8 decoding in `load()` raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, which is neither `ParseError` nor `OSError`. Included files that fail to decode are `cannot read include`, as the spec says.
+- **Platform:** tested only on Linux with Python 3.14.3.
